    {
      "id": "21003bfb-fd13-433b-835f-26817df6abba",
      "question": "What are the four duration categories of in vivo toxicity studies and their standard durations?",
      "answer": "Acute (single dose or ≤24h) → hazard identification; Subacute (28-day) → repeat-dose effects; Subchronic (90-day) → subchronic NOAEL, target organs; Chronic (≥12 months, often 2-year rodent) → lifetime exposure, carcinogenicity, progressive pathology.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-09T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:10:35.796690+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block1",
      "last_user_answer": "Acute <24h, subacute 28d, subchronic 90d, chronic 12mo — but missed exact subchronic=90d and chronic=12mo on first pass"
    },
    {
      "id": "809d0e87-7892-4a44-9f0b-5808a0eb37df",
      "question": "What measure does a cohort study produce and when is it the preferred design?",
      "answer": "Relative Risk (RR). Preferred when the EXPOSURE is rare (can select exposed cohort). Estimates incidence. Weakness: expensive, long follow-up, loss to follow-up.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-09T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:10:35.796690+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block1",
      "last_user_answer": "Cohort is exposure and effects, backwards looking — missed RR, missed direction, missed when preferred"
    },
    {
      "id": "72768355-b706-449e-a534-f8834d373118",
      "question": "What measure does a case-control study produce and when is it the preferred design?",
      "answer": "Odds Ratio (OR). Preferred when the OUTCOME is rare (e.g., specific cancer). Faster/cheaper than cohort. Weakness: recall bias, control selection critical, cannot estimate incidence directly.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-11T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:10:35.796690+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block1",
      "last_user_answer": "Case control generates incidents — confused with cohort, missed OR, missed outcome-rare criterion"
    },
    {
      "id": "cef7965d-33f4-4fd8-bb7b-9b138e39f614",
      "question": "What is the standard dose spacing pattern for repeated-dose toxicity studies in OECD guidelines and why?",
      "answer": "Logarithmic spacing — typically ~2× to ~3.2× intervals between dose groups. Minimum 3 dose groups + vehicle control. Log spacing captures dose-response across orders of magnitude without wasting animals on narrow, uninformative ranges.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-07T23:09:44.639745+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:10:35.796690+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block1",
      "last_user_answer": "Logarithmic 2x or 3x, proper spacing to get idea of toxic level — correct pattern, reasoning slightly vague"
    },
    {
      "id": "68df6431-5ef3-42cf-b4cf-624d194404f6",
      "question": "Describe the cross-sectional study design, its measure, key weakness, and appropriate use.",
      "answer": "Snapshot at one time point — exposure and outcome measured simultaneously. Measure: Prevalence ratio. Key weakness: temporal ambiguity (cannot establish causation — which came first?). Appropriate use: hypothesis generation, estimating disease burden, quick initial assessment.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-09T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:10:35.796690+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block1",
      "last_user_answer": "Large population across groups, measures incidence, weakness is sheer variables — missed prevalence, missed temporal ambiguity, missed snapshot design"
    },
    {
      "id": "388c240e-e02e-4336-8c0e-98f164a0f66b",
      "question": "In the stomach (pH 2), which form of a weak acid (pKa 4) can cross membranes: the non-ionized (HA) or ionized (A⁻) form?",
      "answer": "Non-ionized (HA). Weak acids are non-ionized in acidic environments → can cross lipid membranes. Henderson-Hasselbalch: acid pH → unionized acid form predominates.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-07T23:12:53.747105+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:52:16.905101+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block2-adme",
      "last_user_answer": "Non-ionized form — correct"
    },
    {
      "id": "a7f2bf0d-2197-4772-af87-4ad7b28cc601",
      "question": "A weak base (pKa 8.5) is administered orally. Does it absorb better in the stomach (pH 2) or small intestine (pH 6.5)?",
      "answer": "Small intestine (pH 6.5). Weak bases are protonated (ionized, charged) in acidic stomach → can't cross. At higher pH, the non-ionized fraction increases → absorption improves.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-07T23:17:33.222776+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:52:16.905101+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block2-adme",
      "last_user_answer": "Small intestine because charged in stomach — correct"
    },
    {
      "id": "e113e3aa-82b2-4afa-8ef3-372279572f6e",
      "question": "What happens to oral bioavailability (F) of a high hepatic extraction ratio (E > 0.7) compound when the dose is increased enough to saturate first-pass metabolism?",
      "answer": "Bioavailability (F) increases disproportionately. Normally high E → low F (most drug stripped in one pass). Saturation of first-pass → more escapes into systemic circulation → nonlinear PK with supraproportional AUC increase.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-07T23:19:10.730485+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:52:16.905101+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block2-adme",
      "last_user_answer": "Bioavailability will increase by a lot — direction correct, missing disproportionate/supraproportional precision"
    },
    {
      "id": "0542e825-ee66-4b65-85fc-6641169e9cf6",
      "question": "What is the difference between Phase I and Phase II metabolism in terms of what they do to a toxicant?",
      "answer": "Phase I = functionalization — adds or exposes a polar group (–OH, –NH₂, –COOH) via oxidation/reduction/hydrolysis (CYP450s). Phase II = conjugation — attaches a water-soluble group (glucuronide, sulfate, glutathione) for excretion. Phase I can bioactivate (make MORE toxic); Phase II almost always detoxifies.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-07T23:20:38.553261+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:52:16.905101+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block2-adme",
      "last_user_answer": "Phase I modifies and can increase toxicity, Phase II reduces toxicity — correct concept"
    },
    {
      "id": "548ed302-907d-408a-bb6d-c629c462af80",
      "question": "If a toxicant is 95% plasma protein-bound at low doses, what happens to the free (unbound) fraction when binding sites become saturated at high doses?",
      "answer": "Free fraction increases — every additional mg of dose goes into the unbound pool. Only unbound toxicant can leave blood, reach tissues, and be eliminated by glomerular filtration. Saturation of binding is a nonlinear PK source.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-07T23:22:32.036872+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:52:16.905101+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block2-adme",
      "last_user_answer": "Free unbound fraction increases by a lot — correct, missing nonlinear PK framing"
    },
    {
      "id": "9b841d4a-257f-4d60-9eac-d88f7b738c69",
      "question": "True or false: A compound with a very high Volume of Distribution (e.g., 50 L/kg) is rapidly eliminated because it distributes widely.",
      "answer": "FALSE. High Vd means the compound is sequestered in peripheral tissues (fat, bone) and must slowly redistribute back to the central compartment before it can be eliminated. High Vd → LONG t½, not fast elimination. Common misconception trap.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 1,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-11T23:23:14.052328+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:52:16.905101+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block2-adme",
      "last_user_answer": "False — high Vd means sequestered, elimination very slow — correct"
    },
    {
      "id": "75673456-7e66-459c-80f2-c2eaaf2142c0",
      "question": "If hepatic clearance decreases by half (due to liver injury), what happens to the elimination half-life? Name which variable in t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL changed.",
      "answer": "t½ doubles (increases 2×). CL is the variable that decreased. Since t½ = 0.693 × Vd / CL, halving CL doubles t½ (inverse relationship, Vd constant).",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 1,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-11T23:23:57.528691+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:52:16.905101+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block2-adme",
      "last_user_answer": "CL is the variable, halving CL doubles t½ — correct math and reasoning"
    },
    {
      "id": "b2ae2255-80b7-4b48-8ebc-c82ed759bbdd",
      "question": "Using the four-compartment TK model (GI tract → Central → Peripheral → Elimination), why does a lipophilic compound with Vd = 100 L/kg have a half-life measured in years?",
      "answer": "Absorbed compound moves: GI → Central (blood) → rapidly distributes into Peripheral (fat, deep tissues). Most of the dose is in Peripheral, not Central. Elimination organs (kidney, liver) can only clear what's in Central blood. The rate-limiting step is slow redistribution from Peripheral back to Central → extremely long t½ (years for TCDD, PCBs).",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-07T23:25:30.243547+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-28T23:52:16.905101+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block2-adme",
      "last_user_answer": "Lipophilic in fat, hard to get out — correct concept, missing rate-limiting step = Peripheral to Central redistribution"
    },
    {
      "id": "b67b8366-16fc-41da-9277-c3e859006abc",
      "question": "What can in vitro toxicity studies NOT tell you? Name at least 3 limitations.",
      "answer": "In vitro studies CANNOT tell you: (1) ADME — absorption/distribution/metabolism/excretion in a whole organism, (2) systemic toxicity or dose-response in an intact system, (3) complex feedback loops (endocrine, immune, neural).",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-07T23:26:30.478994+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "Clinical outcomes, organ systems, imperfect view — correct concept, missed ADME terminology, confused mechanism limitation with mechanism strength"
    },
    {
      "id": "2e969eca-7ba4-49cc-909a-cbadbd1635cc",
      "question": "When designing a chronic (2-year) bioassay from a 90-day subchronic study with NOAEL=50 mg/kg, should the chronic high dose be higher or lower than the subchronic high dose? Why?",
      "answer": "LOWER. Chronic exposure is longer — cumulative damage amplifies. Chronic high dose should be below the subchronic LOAEL, often around the subchronic NOAEL or lower. Common exam trap: \"longer study = need higher doses\" is backward.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 1,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-11T23:27:43.716590+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "Lower — cumulative damage, animals would die early — correct with strong reasoning"
    },
    {
      "id": "85bd3a79-a22a-4dbc-91f3-d55a72dcb139",
      "question": "What is the standard limit dose for repeated-dose toxicity studies, and when is it used?",
      "answer": "1000 mg/kg/day (sometimes 2000). The limit dose is the highest dose tested when no toxicity is expected. If no effects at the limit dose, no higher doses are needed.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-09T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "Limit dose is one thousand mg per kg"
    },
    {
      "id": "69c4ea9a-d603-4f6d-b4bb-02f5088f1437",
      "question": "What is the standard dose spacing pattern for repeated-dose toxicity studies?",
      "answer": "Logarithmic spacing at 3.2x or 2x intervals. At least 3 dose groups plus a vehicle control are required by OECD TG guidelines.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-11T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": null
    },
    {
      "id": "0c498add-2762-473b-b6fc-bf6f209b4ff5",
      "question": "What risk metric does a cohort study produce, and what scenario is it best for?",
      "answer": "Relative Risk (RR). Best for rare EXPOSURES — you deliberately select an exposed cohort and follow forward. Weakness: expensive, long follow-up, loss to follow-up.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-10T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "Risk ratio - good for backwards looking exposure"
    },
    {
      "id": "37c4163c-7600-4a36-b9a8-9f280f812168",
      "question": "What risk metric does a case-control study produce, and what scenario is it best for?",
      "answer": "Odds Ratio (OR). Best for rare OUTCOMES — you select cases and look backward at exposure history. Weakness: recall bias, control selection is critical.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-10T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": null
    },
    {
      "id": "f935a5f4-46fa-49e2-b606-7f08a219ef0d",
      "question": "What is the key weakness of cross-sectional studies in epidemiology?",
      "answer": "Temporal ambiguity — exposure and outcome measured simultaneously, so you CANNOT establish causation (only association). Measures prevalence ratio.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-08T21:46:14.747349+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "Temporal ambiguity - cannot establish causation because exposure and outcome measured simultaneously"
    },
    {
      "id": "277595ed-521c-49b4-a897-4df39298773f",
      "question": "Which routes of administration bypass first-pass hepatic metabolism?",
      "answer": "Sublingual, intravenous, inhalation, dermal, and rectal (partial). Oral administration is subject to first-pass metabolism via the portal vein to the liver.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-10T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "IV"
    },
    {
      "id": "acebddf2-6560-4bd0-b84e-759fe319ba09",
      "question": "What is first-pass metabolism and how does it affect oral bioavailability (F)?",
      "answer": "Drug absorbed from GI tract passes through the liver via portal vein before systemic circulation. Hepatic extraction ratio (E) determines F: F = 1 - E. High E — low F — saturable at high doses causing nonlinear PK.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-08T21:47:15.616700+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "Metabolized before systemic circulation via portal vein - decreases oral F"
    },
    {
      "id": "e9c45ebf-a184-49c5-b78c-21eba7bceaac",
      "question": "What is the difference between CYP450 induction and CYP450 inhibition?",
      "answer": "INDUCTION = increased enzyme synthesis (takes days, e.g., ethanol inducing CYP2E1). INHIBITION = decreased enzyme activity (takes hours, e.g., ketoconazole inhibiting CYP3A4). Both alter clearance and cause drug interactions.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-10T16:24:35.323886+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "INDUCTION = increased enzyme synthesis (takes days, e.g., ethanol inducing CYP2E1). INHIBITION = decreased enzyme activity (takes hours, e.g., ketoconazole inhibiting CYP3A4). Both alter clearance and cause drug interactions."
    },
    {
      "id": "83ba4045-1b51-46f9-8af9-11bdb4dd9292",
      "question": "Describe the acetaminophen (APAP) toxification pathway. What makes it hepatotoxic at high doses?",
      "answer": "Therapeutic dose: Phase II conjugation (glucuronidation/sulfation) — safe. Overdose: Phase II saturated, Phase I (CYP2E1/CYP3A4) converts APAP to NAPQI (reactive metabolite). NAPQI depletes glutathione — binds hepatic proteins — liver necrosis. Antidote: N-acetylcysteine (replenishes glutathione).",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-08T21:48:30.257237+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "Phase II saturates at overdose, CYP2E1/3A4 converts APAP to NAPQI, GSH depleted, liver necrosis. Antidote NAC."
    },
    {
      "id": "ebbf8840-d614-4593-b395-49eec92db4b1",
      "question": "What is the formula for Volume of Distribution (Vd), and does a high Vd mean fast or slow elimination?",
      "answer": "Vd = Dose / C0 (initial plasma concentration). High Vd — SLOW elimination. The drug is sequestered in peripheral tissues (fat, muscle) and must redistribute back to central compartment before it can be cleared. Common trap: high Vd does NOT mean fast elimination.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-08T21:49:06.127230+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "High Vd = slow elimination, formula Vd = Dose/C0"
    },
    {
      "id": "e4fd52f2-a5ea-4d2d-ab4a-26311c62fedb",
      "question": "What is enterohepatic recirculation and how does it affect half-life?",
      "answer": "Compound excreted in bile into intestine, then reabsorbed back into portal circulation. This recycling prolongs half-life significantly. Examples: PCBs, methylmercury, some steroids. Interrupting this (e.g., Prussian blue for thallium) enhances elimination.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-08T21:49:59.614137+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "Excreted in bile, gut bacteria deconjugate, reabsorbed into portal circulation - prolongs half-life"
    },
    {
      "id": "3ecc64ec-b400-4e1d-b080-258306f5ebb5",
      "question": "What are the three main routes of xenobiotic excretion?",
      "answer": "Renal (urine) — handles most water-soluble compounds via glomerular filtration + tubular secretion. Biliary (feces) — handles larger molecules (>300 Da) and conjugates. Pulmonary — handles volatile compounds (gases, solvents, anesthetic agents).",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-08T21:51:17.207782+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T20:04:30.849042+00:00",
      "video_id": "domain-i-blocks1-2-supplement",
      "last_user_answer": "Renal (urine), biliary (feces), pulmonary (exhalation)"
    },
    {
      "id": "624fecf4-0fd0-4cad-9721-7cf7db25ff3a",
      "question": "Why is only the free (unbound) fraction of a drug pharmacologically active?",
      "answer": "Only unbound drug can: (1) cross membranes to reach target tissues, (2) be filtered by glomerulus for renal elimination, (3) interact with receptors. Protein-bound drug is a reservoir — as free drug is eliminated, bound drug dissociates to maintain equilibrium.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
