      "last_user_answer": null
    },
    {
      "id": "b392a234-d128-400a-b448-b92e427d16aa",
      "question": "A study has 5 dose groups vs. control. The toxicologist uses Bonferroni correction (α/5 = 0.01) for post-hoc comparisons. What is the main problem with this approach?",
      "answer": "Bonferroni treats all 5 comparisons as independent, but they aren't — they all share the same control group. Dunnett's test is the standard post-hoc for multiple dose groups vs. a single control because it accounts for this correlation, giving a less conservative threshold (more power to detect real effects).",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-10T16:33:39.067308+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T21:36:34.241739+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block3-stats",
      "last_user_answer": "Bonferroni treats all 5 comparisons as independent, but they aren't — they all share the same control group. Dunnett's test is the standard post-hoc for multiple dose groups vs. a single control because it accounts for this correlation, giving a less conservative threshold (more power to detect real effects)."
    },
    {
      "id": "b58a07cb-47db-438e-a5d9-05092e2bb257",
      "question": "What are the four parameters that determine a study's statistical power, and how do they trade off?",
      "answer": "Sample size (n), effect size, α (Type I error rate), and power (1 − β). Trade-off: ↑ n → ↑ power; ↑ effect size → ↑ power; ↑ α → ↑ power but more false positives. Fix any three, the fourth is determined. Underpowered studies (e.g., 5/group) cannot reliably detect moderate effects.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-09T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T21:36:34.241739+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block3-stats",
      "last_user_answer": null
    },
    {
      "id": "4578042d-b8b7-4a7d-873f-1fc50b2dd730",
      "question": "What does 'not statistically significant' (p > 0.05) in a toxicology study NOT mean?",
      "answer": "It does NOT mean: (1) the chemical is safe, (2) there was no effect, (3) the effect was small. It only means: the study failed to reject the null hypothesis at α = 0.05. In an underpowered study, a true effect can produce p > 0.05 — the most dangerous misinterpretation in toxicology.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-09T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T21:36:34.241739+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block3-stats",
      "last_user_answer": null
    },
    {
      "id": "e3e8540f-3fc6-4a5d-b69a-65427b315d3f",
      "question": "What differentiates a paired t-test from an independent (2-sample) t-test, and when is each used in toxicology?",
      "answer": "Paired t-test: same subjects measured before/after (e.g., BP pre-dose vs. post-dose in same dog). More powerful because it controls for inter-subject variability. Independent t-test: separate groups of animals (e.g., treated vs. control). ABT trap: using paired when groups are independent, or using unpaired when a pre/post design was used.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-10T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T21:36:34.241739+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block3-stats",
      "last_user_answer": null
    },
    {
      "id": "4df76be0-8705-41a5-a322-2de5e73c69bd",
      "question": "Continuous data fail normality in a 4-group study. Which non-parametric test replaces ANOVA, and which replaces Dunnett?",
      "answer": "Kruskal-Wallis test (non-parametric ANOVA substitute — compares medians via ranks). Post-hoc: Dunn's test (non-parametric substitute for Dunnett/Tukey). Do NOT run parametric ANOVA on non-normal data — it violates the normality of residuals assumption, distorting the F-statistic.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-08T12:00:00+00:00",
      "created_at": "2026-05-29T21:36:34.241739+00:00",
      "video_id": "conduct-of-studies-block3-stats",
      "last_user_answer": null
    },
    {
      "id": "27f14149-b115-4aeb-91a6-a4d5009d538c",
      "question": "What is the key difference between Williams' test and Dunnett's test for continuous endpoints in repeated-dose studies?",
      "answer": "Williams' test assumes monotonic dose-response (effect only increases with dose), which gives more power to detect a dose-related effect when the assumption holds. Dunnett's does not assume monotonicity — it compares each dose to control independently. OECD guidance recommends Williams as the primary trend test.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
      "next_review_at": "2026-06-08T12:00:00+00:00",
