      "last_user_answer": null
    },
    {
      "id": "f0498684-52c2-443b-847f-6e307338d673",
      "question": "A toxicology report states 'mean ± SE' for a group of 10 animals. What additional information do you need to assess whether the reported variability reflects actual animal-to-animal variation?",
      "answer": "You need the SD (or the n). SE = SD/√n, so the actual animal variability is SD = SE × √n. If mean ± SE = 68 ± 21 and n = 10, the SD ≈ 66 — meaning individual values span roughly 0–200 U/L, not 47–89 as the SE might suggest. Always check whether SD or SE is reported.",
      "collection": "DABT - Conduct of Studies",
      "status": "learning",
      "ease_streak": 0,
